---

# Node Types

Three node types form the building blocks of a tunnel. Entry and exit are required — relays are optional and only needed when pivoting through intermediate hosts.

### Entry

The entry node runs on the local machine. A [TUN interface](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TUN/TAP) (a virtual network device) is established to route traffic into the tunnel chain.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :PORT" /%}

### Relay *(optional)*

An intermediate host forwards traffic deeper into the network. Relays connect to a previous hop and listen for a subsequent hop.

{% codeblock code="wallhack relay --connect HOST:PORT --listen :PORT" /%}

### Exit

The far end of the tunnel chain. Packets are unwrapped and delivered to the target network.

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect HOST:PORT" /%}

Transport direction (`--listen` / `--connect`) is independent of node role — entry and exit nodes can either listen or connect, which is how reverse tunnels work.

